WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.100 --> 00:00:02.400
-We love you! Come on.
-No, no!

00:00:02.400 --> 00:00:04.234
-Let's get into everything
that you've been doing.

00:00:04.234 --> 00:00:06.033
-No.
-First of all, you're hosting

00:00:06.033 --> 00:00:07.100
"Saturday Night" --
-No!

00:00:07.100 --> 00:00:09.234
-You!
No, you!

00:00:09.234 --> 00:00:12.367
-No, you.
-It is all you right now.

00:00:12.367 --> 00:00:14.267
-What?
-"Saturday Night Live."

00:00:14.267 --> 00:00:16.200
You're hosting it this weekend
with Sam Smith.

00:00:16.200 --> 00:00:17.334
Unbelievable.
-I know.

00:00:17.334 --> 00:00:19.067
-This is a giant deal.
-I know.

00:00:19.067 --> 00:00:20.968
-Are you excited?
-I'm scared.

00:00:20.968 --> 00:00:22.834
Hold me.
-No. No, you're not scared.

00:00:22.834 --> 00:00:24.501
-Hold me!
-You actually --

00:00:24.501 --> 00:00:26.934
You used to intern there.
-I did.

00:00:26.934 --> 00:00:30.300
I was an intern
in the design department

00:00:30.300 --> 00:00:32.734
for the four set designers,
who I know you're aware of.

00:00:32.734 --> 00:00:35.567
-I do love the designers.
-Leo.

00:00:35.567 --> 00:00:38.067
-Yeah, Leo Yoshimora, yeah.
-Eugene.

00:00:38.067 --> 00:00:39.400
-Eugene, of course.
-And Keith.

00:00:39.400 --> 00:00:41.300
-And Keith Raywood.
I love them all, they're great.

00:00:41.300 --> 00:00:42.701
-Uh-huh.
They loved me.

00:00:42.701 --> 00:00:44.067
They loved me because, um,

00:00:44.067 --> 00:00:45.968
I did not care about
set design at all.

00:00:45.968 --> 00:00:47.133
[ Laughter ]

00:00:47.133 --> 00:00:49.300
And they wanted an intern
that had no interest

00:00:49.300 --> 00:00:51.267
in learning what they did,

00:00:51.267 --> 00:00:53.300
because they were
moody bastards.

00:00:53.300 --> 00:00:54.567
[ Laughter ]

00:00:54.567 --> 00:00:56.267
And you know that's true.
-They are.

00:00:56.267 --> 00:00:58.601
-I mean, I love them, but --
-They are.

00:00:58.601 --> 00:01:00.200
They are moody.
-They are.

00:01:00.200 --> 00:01:02.567
In fact, their office
was labelled "The Moody Room."

00:01:02.567 --> 00:01:03.901
-It was, actually.
Yeah, yeah.

00:01:03.901 --> 00:01:05.634
They know what they're doing.
-They were so grumpy.

00:01:05.634 --> 00:01:07.601
-Well, you weren't there when
I was there, though, right?

00:01:07.601 --> 00:01:10.000
-I was there 2004, 2005.

00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:11.334
-Oh, that's when I left.
Yeah.

00:01:11.334 --> 00:01:13.033
-Um, yeah, I know.
I replaced you.

00:01:13.033 --> 00:01:14.133
[ Laughter ]
-Oh, that's right.

00:01:14.133 --> 00:01:15.968
Congratulations.
They picked perfectly.

00:01:15.968 --> 00:01:17.200
-They had to get
the energy shift.

00:01:17.200 --> 00:01:18.667
-Who was on the cast?

00:01:18.667 --> 00:01:20.133
Did you get to talk
to the cast at all?

00:01:20.133 --> 00:01:22.067
-I didn't really talk
to the cast 'cause I was --

00:01:22.067 --> 00:01:23.868
I was -- you know,
I was an intern.

00:01:23.868 --> 00:01:26.868
I was, like, kind of stalking,
lurking in the shadows.

00:01:26.868 --> 00:01:28.167
-But you studied
the whole thing.

00:01:28.167 --> 00:01:30.234
You go, "Oh, this is great."
-I studied like a sponge.

00:01:30.234 --> 00:01:34.000
I was just, like, creepy.
Like, I was a creepy stalker.

00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:37.100
Like, now I'm gonna host it,
so my master plan worked.

00:01:37.100 --> 00:01:38.567
[ Laughter ]
-Oh, my God.

00:01:38.567 --> 00:01:41.434
But you actually got to audition
also for "Saturday Night Live."

00:01:41.434 --> 00:01:43.567
-I did.
I -- I didn't do, like --

00:01:43.567 --> 00:01:46.133
I didn't make it to the bit --

00:01:46.133 --> 00:01:48.434
like, to the Lorne audition,
you know,

00:01:48.434 --> 00:01:50.601
the famous final audition,

00:01:50.601 --> 00:01:54.601
but I did, like, a preliminary
first-round showcase at ECB.

00:01:54.601 --> 00:01:56.033
-Did you do characters?

00:01:56.033 --> 00:01:57.267
Did you do, like,
original characters?

00:01:57.267 --> 00:01:59.567
-I did.
I did some characters.

00:01:59.567 --> 00:02:02.200
-Do you remember any of them?
-I remember one character I did

00:02:02.200 --> 00:02:07.267
was, like, a -- kind of like
a Puerto Rican, like,

00:02:07.267 --> 00:02:09.534
news reporter that was always
trying to, like,

00:02:09.534 --> 00:02:12.868
make all of
the news stories sexy,

00:02:12.868 --> 00:02:14.501
even if they were, like,
horrific news stories.

00:02:14.501 --> 00:02:16.000
[ Laughter ]

00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:18.968
And I was just trying to, like,
sex up the news or something.

00:02:18.968 --> 00:02:20.968
I don't remember.
And then the other one was,

00:02:20.968 --> 00:02:23.067
I was, like,
a pill-popping housewife

00:02:23.067 --> 00:02:26.000
that had my own talk show,
called "Celebri-tails,"

00:02:26.000 --> 00:02:27.567
where I would just
name celebrities

00:02:27.567 --> 00:02:30.000
and name what kind of tail
they would have if they were --

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:33.067
if they had a tail.
[ Laughter ]

00:02:33.067 --> 00:02:36.534
Like, I would say, like,
"Lindsay Lohan would have, like,

00:02:36.534 --> 00:02:39.033
a bushy squirrel's tail."
[ Laughter ]

00:02:39.033 --> 00:02:42.667
Or, like, "Bill Clinton would
have, like, a polar bear's nub."

00:02:42.667 --> 00:02:45.033
[ Laughter ]
Or, like, Oprah Winfrey would --

00:02:45.033 --> 00:02:47.033
-Oh, my gosh.
-Just stuff like that.

00:02:47.033 --> 00:02:48.300
Anyway, I didn't
get on the show, so...

00:02:48.300 --> 00:02:50.033
-No. But here you are.
-I know.

00:02:50.033 --> 00:02:51.334
-I mean, "Saturday Night Live."
Hosting "Saturday --"

00:02:51.334 --> 00:02:54.501
I haven't seen you since
you were on for "White Lotus."

00:02:54.501 --> 00:02:56.567
-Yeah.
-You were unbelievable.

00:02:56.567 --> 00:02:57.901
-Thank you.
-It was amazing.

00:02:57.901 --> 00:02:58.901
-Thank you.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:02:58.901 --> 00:03:01.033
You were fantastic.

00:03:01.033 --> 00:03:03.033
I couldn't take
my eyes off of you.

00:03:03.033 --> 00:03:04.400
You nailed it.
-Yeah.

00:03:04.400 --> 00:03:06.033
-You crushed it.
My wife was like, "Oh, my gosh.

00:03:06.033 --> 00:03:08.434
Aubrey Plaza's un--"
We worshipped you in our house.

00:03:08.434 --> 00:03:09.601
-Thank you.
-We were just like,

00:03:09.601 --> 00:03:11.300
"What is going down?"
You had it.

00:03:11.300 --> 00:03:13.901
I'm like, "Oh, my gosh.
You got every nomination."

00:03:13.901 --> 00:03:15.634
The show got a Golden Globe.
-I know.

00:03:15.634 --> 00:03:17.067
-The cast got nominated
for a SAG award.

00:03:17.067 --> 00:03:18.334
-I know. Jennifer Coolidge,
racking it up.

00:03:18.334 --> 00:03:21.267
-Jennifer Coolidge won.
I mean, the whole cast.

00:03:21.267 --> 00:03:24.167
And you, buddy.
You were the secret sauce.

00:03:24.167 --> 00:03:25.634
You're the glue.
-Oh, I was barely there.

00:03:25.634 --> 00:03:27.467
I was barely there.
-No, my gosh.

00:03:27.467 --> 00:03:29.000
It was just unbelievable.

00:03:29.000 --> 00:03:30.534
-I was just trying
to save my marriage.

00:03:30.534 --> 00:03:31.567
-No.
[ Laughter ]

00:03:31.567 --> 00:03:33.300
Exactly right, exactly.

00:03:33.300 --> 00:03:34.901
So you had "White Lotus"
come out,

00:03:34.901 --> 00:03:36.667
and it couldn't
have been better.

00:03:36.667 --> 00:03:38.100
Man, again, thanks,
congrats on that.

00:03:38.100 --> 00:03:39.400
And thanks for being here.
-Yeah.

00:03:39.400 --> 00:03:41.033
-Then you also had
"Emily the Criminal"...

00:03:41.033 --> 00:03:43.000
-Yes.
-...which is fantastic.

00:03:43.000 --> 00:03:44.801
This is another one
that's getting nominated.

00:03:44.801 --> 00:03:46.834
Awards everywhere.
In fact, look at this --

00:03:46.834 --> 00:03:49.300
Barack Obama's
favorite movie's of 2022.

00:03:49.300 --> 00:03:51.000
-I know. We got Obama!
-Look at this --

00:03:51.000 --> 00:03:52.667
"Emily the Criminal."
-Yes.

00:03:52.667 --> 00:03:54.667
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:03:54.667 --> 00:03:55.901
Yeah.

00:03:55.901 --> 00:03:58.133
-Come on!
-I know.

00:03:58.133 --> 00:04:00.267
-How great is this?
-I don't know.

00:04:00.267 --> 00:04:01.300
-It's so exciting.

00:04:01.300 --> 00:04:02.634
-I wonder if Joe Biden
watched it.

00:04:02.634 --> 00:04:03.868
-Yes, he probably saw it, too.
[ Laughter ]

00:04:03.868 --> 00:04:05.400
-Come on, Joe.
-Yeah, come on, Joe.

00:04:05.400 --> 00:04:07.100
Come on, dude. Just say
you liked "Emily the Criminal."

00:04:07.100 --> 00:04:08.133
-I know.

00:04:08.133 --> 00:04:10.133
-The reviews on this
are fantastic.

00:04:10.133 --> 00:04:12.501
94% on Rotten Tomatoes.

00:04:12.501 --> 00:04:14.067
You actually produced it,
as well, right?

00:04:14.067 --> 00:04:16.434
-I did, yes.
I had my hands all over it.

00:04:16.434 --> 00:04:17.567
-Wow.
-Yeah.

00:04:17.567 --> 00:04:19.334
-Can we set up the --
he idea of

00:04:19.334 --> 00:04:20.667
"Emily the Criminal"
for everybody?

00:04:20.667 --> 00:04:24.968
-Yeah. So it's a --
it's about a woman named Emily,

00:04:24.968 --> 00:04:27.901
who I play,
who is from New Jersey.

00:04:27.901 --> 00:04:29.868
She's in Los Angeles
trying to make it

00:04:29.868 --> 00:04:31.901
after having a criminal record.

00:04:31.901 --> 00:04:35.567
She has, like, a misdemeanor
felony charge from her past.

00:04:35.567 --> 00:04:37.801
And she's kind of saddled
with student debt

00:04:37.801 --> 00:04:42.100
and trying to just navigate this
broken system that we're in.

00:04:42.100 --> 00:04:45.000
And she basically
gets tipped off

00:04:45.000 --> 00:04:47.534
to this kind of
credit-card-scam operation.

00:04:47.534 --> 00:04:49.067
-Yeah.
What is it called? Dummy?

00:04:49.067 --> 00:04:50.701
-Dummy Shopper.
-Dummy Shopper.

00:04:50.701 --> 00:04:52.567
-And she decides to try it out,

00:04:52.567 --> 00:04:54.567
and it turns out
she's really good at it.

00:04:54.567 --> 00:04:56.100
So, she just --
-Almost too good.

00:04:56.100 --> 00:04:57.734
-Almost too good.
So, she keeps kind of

00:04:57.734 --> 00:05:01.567
going down this rabbit hole
of kind of crime.

00:05:01.567 --> 00:05:04.968
And then she just becomes, like,
a full-blown criminal.

00:05:04.968 --> 00:05:06.234
-Yeah.
-Um, so it's --

00:05:06.234 --> 00:05:08.167
Yeah, it's a thriller.
-It's a thriller.

00:05:08.167 --> 00:05:09.367
It's fantastic.
You're great in it.

00:05:09.367 --> 00:05:10.667
-Thank you.
-I also wanted to mention, too,

00:05:10.667 --> 00:05:12.734
that I didn't even realize --
'cause the movie's over,

00:05:12.734 --> 00:05:13.934
and I was reading reviews
about it.

00:05:13.934 --> 00:05:16.934
There's no gun violence in this.
-There's no guns.

00:05:16.934 --> 00:05:18.400
Yeah, there are no guns
in the movie.

00:05:18.400 --> 00:05:21.901
I think that's a really cool,
kind of weird thing

00:05:21.901 --> 00:05:25.067
about the film, because most
thrillers introduce guns,

00:05:25.067 --> 00:05:26.934
like, immediately.
-I didn't even think about it.

00:05:26.934 --> 00:05:28.567
-I know.
I don't think it's like --

00:05:28.567 --> 00:05:30.000
it's an unconscious thing,
I think, for the audience

00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:31.901
to watch that movie
and not even realize,

00:05:31.901 --> 00:05:34.400
like, "Oh, there's no guns."
But I think it actually makes it

00:05:34.400 --> 00:05:37.133
more, kind of, anxiety-inducing,
because you're like,

00:05:37.133 --> 00:05:39.634
"How is she gonna
take these guys down

00:05:39.634 --> 00:05:41.200
without, like,
a weapon like that?"

00:05:41.200 --> 00:05:43.400
But, um,
I was kind of proud of that.

00:05:43.400 --> 00:05:44.701
-Yeah.
-'Cause I don't think

00:05:44.701 --> 00:05:47.501
you need guns -- we don't need
guns in every movie.

00:05:47.501 --> 00:05:48.667
-You did it.
Yeah, exactly.

00:05:48.667 --> 00:05:50.234
Come on. Why not?
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:05:50.234 --> 00:05:51.601
Put a little extra work
in there.

00:05:51.601 --> 00:05:53.501
Make it -- come on, make it --
it's great.

00:05:53.501 --> 00:05:55.567
I want to show a clip.
Here's Aubrey Plaza

00:05:55.567 --> 00:05:58.234
in "Emily the Criminal."
Take a look at this.

00:05:58.234 --> 00:06:02.033
-Can you tell us what happened
with the assault?

00:06:08.200 --> 00:06:09.968
-No.

00:06:09.968 --> 00:06:11.434
-[ Scoffs, chuckles ]
No?

00:06:11.434 --> 00:06:12.667
-No, why would you trick
somebody like that?

00:06:12.667 --> 00:06:16.701
-I -- oh, I did not trick you.
-Yeah, you did.

00:06:16.701 --> 00:06:18.534
-[ Scoffs ]
What I did is very common.

00:06:18.534 --> 00:06:20.033
That's a very common technique.
-Oh, is that right?

00:06:20.033 --> 00:06:23.334
So you do that all the time?
-If people chose to be honest,

00:06:23.334 --> 00:06:24.701
then I wouldn't have to do that
all the time.

00:06:24.701 --> 00:06:26.267
-Well, you know what?
[Bleep] this.

00:06:26.267 --> 00:06:28.701
I don't even want to work here.
-Emily, I'm sorry you're upset.

00:06:28.701 --> 00:06:30.467
-No, you're not.
-Calm down, please.

00:06:30.467 --> 00:06:32.434
-I am calm. And you ask me
why I want this job?

00:06:32.434 --> 00:06:33.767
'Cause I've got $70,000
of student debt.

00:06:33.767 --> 00:06:34.767
There's your [bleep]
answer right there.

00:06:34.767 --> 00:06:35.834
-All right, yeah,
you need to leave.

00:06:35.834 --> 00:06:36.901
-Actually, you know what?
Give me this.

00:06:36.901 --> 00:06:38.300
-I -- okay, fine, thank you.

00:06:38.300 --> 00:06:40.133
-Yeah, thank you, too.
Excuse me.

00:06:40.133 --> 00:06:41.968
[ Cheers and applause ]
-Aubrey Plaza, everybody!

00:06:41.968 --> 00:06:45.367
"Emily the Criminal"
is streaming now on Netflix.

00:06:45.367 --> 00:06:47.400
We're playing a fun new game
when we come back.

00:06:47.400 --> 00:06:48.968
Stick around, everybody.

